'''Zong''' ([[Giokji]]: 總; [[POJ]]: ''Chóng''), officially the State of Zong ([[Giokji]]: 總國; [[POJ]]: ''Chóngkok''), is a [[w:Sovereign state|sovereign state]] in [[Eastern Fosia]]. It is situated along the eastern seaboard of the [[Fosia|Fosian]] continent, bordering the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of over 393 million people, it is one of the most populous and economically significant nations in Adonia. Zong operates as a unitary parliamentary constitutional monarchy under King [[Kengjun]] and Prime Minister [[Tan Bopo]], with its capital and largest city located at [[Huwei]]. The nation possesses a deep and complex history spanning thousands of years, characterized by ancient philosophical traditions, maritime expansion, and a unique transition from imperial absolutism to a modern democratic state.

[[File:Zong_Capital_Cityscape.jpg|thumb|right|The modern skyline of Huwei, showcasing the economic miracle of the post-war era alongside ancient architectural elements.]]

==History==
{{main|Unification of Zong|Jade Sea War|An-Ming Understanding|Great Adonian War}}

The history of Zong spans over two millennia, evolving from a collection of river valley settlements into one of Adonia's most influential nations. From its foundational role as the progenitor of East Fosian civilization to its modern status as an industrial and maritime hegemon, Zong has profoundly shaped the geopolitical and cultural landscape of the Shendan Ocean basin.

===Etymology===
The name of the state derives from the ancient character for "gather" or "total," historically utilized by early tribes in the [[Jade Peninsula]] to describe the unification of diverse river valley settlements. During the classical era, the term became associated with the centralized authority of the imperial courts. In regional languages such as Shansi Cantonese, the name is pronounced as ''Zung'', while in the widespread Hokkien dialects it is pronounced as ''Chóng''.

===Prehistory and Early Civilizations===
Human habitation in the region dates to the [[w:Paleolithic|Paleolithic]] era, with archaeological evidence suggesting early hominid settlements along the fertile basins of the Jade River and the Pearl River. The earliest agrarian societies emerged around 5000 BCE, cultivating rice and millet. These settlements eventually coalesced into advanced regional cultures, most notably the [[Nakashima Culture]] in the south and the early animist tribes of the north.

The invention of the [[Donghai Script]] around the Jade Peninsula marked the transition from prehistory into recorded antiquity. This early writing system, one of the oldest in Eastern Fosia, paved the way for the region's first organized polities and would later evolve into the standardized Zong characters that spread throughout the Shendan basin.

===Kingdom of Donghai and the Haizei Raids===
{{main|Kingdom of Donghai|Haizei}}

The [[Kingdom of Donghai]] (675 BCE – 285 CE) emerged as the dominant power along the northern coast of what would become Zong. Centered on its capital at Haicheng, Donghai controlled a vast maritime trade network extending from the Jade Peninsula to the archipelagos of the northern Shendan Ocean. The kingdom experienced a golden age between 200 BCE and 100 CE, during which it developed advanced shipbuilding techniques and established tributary relationships with coastal communities throughout the region.

However, Donghai's prosperity attracted hostile attention. Beginning in the 1st century CE, the [[Haizei]]—a confederation of maritime raiders originating from the islands of [[Yanbaru]] and Hapo—launched devastating raids along Donghai's northern coastal defenses. These raids, characterized by lightning-fast amphibious assaults and the use of highly maneuverable outrigger warships, progressively weakened Donghai's maritime infrastructure and disrupted its trade networks.

The death knell for Donghai came in 285 CE, when a coordinated Zhongyan invasion from the west coincided with a massive Haizei assault on the coast. The dual offensive overwhelmed Donghai's defenses, and the kingdom collapsed after nearly a millennium of existence. The Haizei raids would continue for another century, fundamentally altering the balance of power in the region and compelling coastal communities to develop new defensive strategies.

===The Mabatok Period and Dagit Influence===
{{main|Mabatok|Kingdom of Kabisayaan}}

From approximately 400 CE, the northeastern coastal archipelago of Mabatok fell under the dominion of the [[Kingdom of Kabisayaan]] (later known as [[Dagit]]). This foreign occupation, led by maritime sea-lords practicing the [[Anitism|Anitist]] faith, represented the only sustained period of non-Zhongyan rule over territory that would become part of modern Zong.

Under Dagit administration, Mabatok developed as a hybrid cultural zone where the indigenous Zhongyuan majority lived under a small Dagiti aristocratic minority. The occupation introduced advanced naval technologies and maritime trade practices that would later prove essential to Zong's own naval development. The province was administered through the Kaharian system—a decentralized network of personal loyalties and naval supremacy rather than bureaucratic land-based governance.

Mabatok remained under Dagit control until the resurgence of Zong military power during the unification period. The last Dagit stronghold at Pulohan fell in 1123 CE, marking the complete integration of the archipelago into the expanding Zong state.

===The Eternal Zongic Wars===
{{main|Eternal Zongic Wars}}

The period from approximately 150 BCE to 350 CE was characterized by near-constant internal conflict known as the Eternal Zongic Wars. This era of fragmentation saw the rise and fall of numerous regional dynasties and warlord states, preventing the emergence of a unified continental power.

The wars were driven by competition for control of the fertile river valleys and trade routes. Various kingdoms and tribal confederations vied for dominance, with alliances shifting rapidly and borders constantly redrawn. The chaos of this period had profound cultural consequences, including the dispersal of Zong intellectual traditions and administrative practices throughout the Shendan basin as refugees and scholars fled the fighting.

===Unification and the Jade Empire===
{{main|Unification of Zong|Jade Empire}}

The unification of Zong was achieved through three successive dynastic phases that collectively spanned nearly 350 years. The process began with the [[House of Jian]] (c. 900–992 CE), which first consolidated control over the central river valleys and established the administrative framework that would underpin the unified state.

The [[House of Long]] (c. 992–1128 CE) expanded Zong territory northward, incorporating the former Kingdom of Donghai's lands and initiating the conquest of Mabatok. This period saw the adoption of the Societal Assembly—a governing institution borrowed from the southern realm of Nanyang that provided a mechanism for integrating conquered territories and legitimizing Zong rule.

The final phase under the [[House of Tian]] (c. 1128–1250 CE) completed the unification process. Under the leadership of King Tianwu and his successors, Zong forces conquered the remaining independent territories, including the last Dagit holdouts in Mabatok. In 1248, the victorious monarch proclaimed the establishment of the [[Jade Empire]], uniting all Zhongyuan territories under a single imperial administration for the first time in history.

===Golden Age of Zong===
{{main|Golden Age of Zong|Great Zhongyuan}}

The century following unification (c. 1230–1300) is remembered as the Golden Age of Zong. With internal conflicts resolved and borders secure, the empire experienced unprecedented prosperity. Agricultural production expanded through the implementation of sophisticated irrigation systems, while trade flourished along both terrestrial and maritime routes.

The capital at Huwei grew into one of the largest cities in the world, attracting scholars, artisans, and merchants from across Adonia. The imperial court patronized the arts and sciences, leading to advances in astronomy, medicine, and engineering. The Great Zhongyuan—the network of roads, canals, and administrative centers that connected the empire—was completed during this period, facilitating the movement of goods, troops, and information throughout the realm.

The era also saw the codification of Zong legal traditions and the standardization of the Zong script, which had by then spread throughout the Shendan basin as the lingua franca of commerce and administration. The philosophical traditions that emerged during this period—emphasizing social harmony, bureaucratic meritocracy, and the divine mandate of the emperor—would influence governance throughout Eastern Fosia for centuries.

===The Jade Sea War and the Triple Kingdoms Alliance===
{{main|Jade Sea War|Triple Kingdoms Alliance}}

The 14th century brought the greatest existential threat to Zong since the Eternal Zongic Wars. The [[Kingdom of Daras]], a rising power to the west, launched a devastating land-based blockade as part of the [[Jade Sea War]] (1300–1390). Darasian forces severed Zong's overland trade routes to western Fosia and the Mesogean world, strangulating the continental economy and threatening imperial collapse.

In response, Zong forged the Triple Kingdoms Alliance with the [[Kingdom of Fenia]] and the [[Kingdom of Shanjian]]. This coalition represented the first major Fosian defensive pact and demonstrated an unprecedented level of coordination among Eastern Fosian states. The alliance dramatically redirected resources toward maritime defense, constructing a massive naval infrastructure that could circumvent the Darasian land blockade.

The war culminated in the Heping Settlement of 1390, which formally ended the conflict and secured a long-term peace. The Triple Kingdoms emerged victorious, and Zong's transition from a terrestrial to a maritime power was complete. The experience of the Jade Sea War would fundamentally reshape Zong strategic doctrine for the next five centuries.

[[File:Triple_Kingdoms_Fleet.png|thumb|left|A historical reconstruction of the Triple Kingdoms Alliance naval fleet preparing for engagement during the Jade Sea War.]]

===Shendan Expansion and New World Colonization===
{{main|Tamsui|Sinchew|Secret Trade}}

With western land routes blocked by the legacy of the Jade Sea War, Zong turned its attention eastward across the Shendan Ocean. The [[Shendan Expansion]] of the 15th and 16th centuries saw Zong pioneer the discovery and colonization of the [[New World]]—the continents of [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]].

The colony of [[Tamsui]], established on the western coast of Lurandia, became Zong's primary colonial hub. It served as a critical nexus for the Fosian diaspora, integrating displaced merchants, artisans, and the samurai refugees fleeing the Sengoku period in the Sanese archipelago. A second major colony, [[Sinchew]], was established to support plantation agriculture.

The colonial era also introduced a dark chapter in Zong history. To fuel Sinchew's plantation economy, the merchant [[Ong Sam]] initiated the illicit [[Secret Trade]] in the 1580s. This system utilized pirates and mercenaries from [[Yanbaru]] and [[Okaiken|Mitoyagi]] to kidnap peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]], transporting them to Lurandia as enslaved labor. Because the Zong Edict of 1500 had officially banned slavery on the mainland, the trade operated in a legal gray area overseas. The demographic legacy of this trade remains a sensitive historiographical subject.

===The An-Ming Understanding and Colonial Partition===
{{main|An-Ming Understanding}}

As Illypnian powers expanded into the Shendan Ocean following the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564, Zong faced new competition for colonial territory. The [[Sultanate of Maros]], a fellow Fosian power, also maintained extensive holdings in the New World, creating potential for conflict over overlapping claims.

To prevent colonial warfare and manage relations with Western powers, Zong diplomat [[Lim Ming]] and envoy [[Chen An]] negotiated the [[An-Ming Understanding]] with Marosan representatives between 1605 and 1606. Signed in the port city of Haihia, these conventions formally partitioned Lurandia and Abyala into respective Zong and Marosan spheres of influence. The agreement established a legal framework that proved Eastern Fosian states could compete with Western colonial empires on equal footing.

[[File:Haihia_Treaty_Signing.jpg|thumb|right|An illustration of diplomats Lim Ming and Chen An finalizing the terms of the An-Ming Understanding in 1605.]]

===The First War of the Isthmus===
{{main|First War of the Isthmus}}

The early 18th century saw Zong drawn into the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), a conflict sparked by Darasian expansionism and competition for control of strategic overland trade routes. The war represented the first major continental conflict since the Jade Sea War and tested the resilience of the Zong military establishment.

Under the leadership of [[Marshal Lanqiu]], Zong forces successfully defended the western frontier and secured a favorable peace settlement. The conflict reinforced the importance of maintaining strong terrestrial defenses even as Zong continued to emphasize maritime power.

===Internal Reform===

The era saw the gradual introduction of Western technological and administrative innovations, particularly in military organization and industrial development. However, conservative factions resisted rapid modernization, creating tensions that would culminate in the constitutional crisis of the early 19th century.

===Constitutional Transition and the 19th Century===
{{main|Constitutional monarchy of Zong}}

The 19th century brought an end to Zong's relative isolation as Western Iron Envoys—Illypnian naval expeditions—challenged the Fosian order. These powers imposed unequal treaties that stripped away tariff autonomy and forced Zong to modernize its diplomatic and military apparatus.

Rather than suffering the complete collapse experienced by some Fosian states, progressive factions within the Zong establishment initiated a controlled transition to constitutional monarchy in 1827. This transformation preserved the imperial institution while establishing a parliamentary system capable of responding to the challenges of the modern era. The constitutional settlement created a framework for political participation and institutional reform that would govern Zong into the 20th century.

===Darasian Interventions===
{{main|Darasian Civil War|Darasian Socialist Revolution}}

The early 20th century saw Zong intervene decisively in the affairs of its historic rival, Daras. The [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] of 1904–1905 threatened to overthrow the traditional monarchy and establish a radical socialist state on Zong's western border. Zong imposed naval blockades and provided covert support to Darasian royalist forces, contributing to the establishment of the Socialist Republic of Daras under terms favorable to Zong strategic interests.

The intervention continued during the [[Darasian Civil War]] (1905–1908), when Zong provided military and logistical support to Loyalist factions seeking to restore the monarchy. The long-term blockade of Darasian ports during this period demonstrated Zong's continued capacity to project maritime power and influence regional politics.

===The Great Adonian War===
{{main|Great Adonian War|Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding}}

Zong's participation in the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952) was framed as a defense of Fosian resource sovereignty against Illypnian colonial powers. The conflict was a devastating maritime struggle that exhausted the imperial system and depleted the nation's resources.

The war was fought in coordination with Zong's allies under the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] of 1895, which had established a framework for cooperative defense among Fosian powers. Zong naval forces conducted extensive operations throughout the Shendan Ocean, protecting shipping lanes and challenging Illypnian naval supremacy.

The conflict ended with the Armistice of Okami in 1952. While Zong retained its sovereignty and core territories, the war resulted in the loss of most colonial holdings and forced a profound internal political restructuring. The imperial institution was further marginalized, and the transition to a fully democratic parliamentary system accelerated.

===Modern Era===
In the decades following the Great Adonian War, Zong transitioned into an industrial titan, emphasizing maritime dominance and economic partnership over territorial expansion. The current constitution, adopted in 1913 and significantly revised in the post-war period, established the highly robust unitary parliamentary constitutional monarchy that governs today.

Modern Zong remains what it has always been: the "Brain of Fosia." From its ancient role as the progenitor of script and law to its modern status as a digital and industrial hegemon, Zong continues to exert profound influence on the civilizational landscape of the Shendan Ocean. The nation maintains deep economic and cultural ties with neighbors including [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Dagit]], while competing strategically with continental powers over resources and influence in the region.

==Geography==
===Physical Characteristics===
Zong occupies a vast and topographically diverse landmass in Eastern Fosia. The geography is broadly divided into the rugged, mountainous regions of the west and the fertile, densely populated river deltas of the east. The Jade Peninsula juts out into the Shendan Ocean, forming a natural maritime gateway that has dictated the nation's naval history. The eastern seaboard is characterized by deep-water ports and intricate archipelagos.

[[File:Zong_Karst_Mountains.jpg|thumb|right|The breathtaking karst mountain landscapes of the western interior, known for their mist-shrouded peaks and deep river valleys.]]

===Climate===
The country experiences a wide range of climatic conditions due to its vast latitudinal extent. The northern territories experience a [[w:Humid continental climate|humid continental climate]] with freezing winters and warm summers. The central river valleys feature a temperate climate, while the southern coastal regions, including the Cantonese-speaking hubs of San-wui and Tyun-mun, are defined by a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] subject to seasonal monsoons and occasional typhoons.

